---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const repoUrl = 'https://github.com/vizuh/clicktrail-js';
---

<Layout
  title="ClickTrail open source — license, governance, releases"
  description="ClickTrail is MIT licensed and maintained by Vizuh OÜ. Governance, release policy, naming convention, provenance, issue tracker, and support channels."
  canonicalPath="/open-source/"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep">/</span><span aria-current="page">Open source</span>
      </nav>
      <p class="kicker">Open source</p>
      <h1>License, governance, and trust</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        ClickTrail is developed in the open under the vizuh GitHub
        organization, with an ethics policy that binds every submission and
        PR.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>License</h2>
      <p>
        The core engine, packages, and integrations are
        <strong>MIT licensed</strong> &mdash; see the
        <a href={`${repoUrl}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>LICENSE</a> file. The
        WordPress plugin (<code>click-trail-handler</code>) remains
        <strong>GPL-2.0-or-later</strong>; MIT embeds cleanly into GPL.
      </p>

      <h2>Ownership</h2>
      <p>
        ClickTrail is built by <strong>Vizuh O&Uuml;</strong>. FunnelSheet is
        Vizuh's consulting branch. The repository is the shared engine beneath
        the <a href="https://wordpress.org/plugins/click-trail-handler/">WordPress plugin distribution</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Support</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Bugs and feature requests: the public <a href={`${repoUrl}/issues`}>issue tracker</a>.</li>
        <li>Security issues: private <a href={`${repoUrl}/security/advisories/new`}>GitHub Security Advisory</a> only &mdash; configuration as code is treated as security-sensitive.</li>
        <li>Every integration README states maintainer, support contact, release policy, compatibility range, and issue tracker (integration policy rule 9).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Release policy</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The stable surfaces of <code>@vizuh/clicktrail</code> are the root entry point and <code>/browser</code>; <code>/conversation</code>, <code>/agent</code>, <code>/otel</code>, and <code>/apointoo</code> are incubating and can change between minor versions.</li>
        <li>Classifier behavior changes are major semver, by definition; every payload carries <code>schema_version</code> and <code>classifier_version</code>.</li>
        <li>Golden fixtures are the executable spec: where fixtures disagree with docs, fixtures win and docs get fixed.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Governance</h2>
      <p>
        The restructuring plan &mdash; package split, event contract,
        submission ladder, kill criteria &mdash; was ratified by a five-person
        council review with documented minority positions. The ethics policy
        in the event contract binds every submission: violations block the PR
        regardless of technical quality.
      </p>

      <h2>Naming convention</h2>
      <p>
        All ClickTrail GitHub repositories are brand-first:
        <code>clicktrail-&lt;ecosystem&gt;</code> (for example
        <code>clicktrail-js</code>, <code>clicktrail-gtm-event-tag</code>,
        <code>clicktrail-shopware</code>). Ecosystem package names keep their
        platform conventions inside each repo; only the repository name
        follows the rule.
      </p>

      <h2>Provenance</h2>
      <p>
        Every integration README documents ownership and provenance per the
        binding integration policy: no self-telemetry, no tracking by default,
        no bundled commercial dependency that cannot be disabled, and secrets
        never embedded in packages &mdash; configuration only. Development
        happens in <a href={repoUrl}>vizuh/clicktrail-js</a>; PRs and issues go there.
      </p>

      <p style="margin-top: 32px;">
        Evaluate the engine itself on the
        <a href="/how-it-works/">how it works page</a>, or start from the
        <a href="/">home page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
